A||N||24||Seanh||Why do I still get horrible gas milage?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||03-29-2001||01:38 AM||||I know you can't just outright answer the question, but what little problems would be likly to give me around 12mpg city? I thought I would get great milage after the upperend rebuild, but nope. I am getting slightly better milage then before, but not by much. I am getting 12 mpg city now. I have a new map sensor, new (Rodney's) vaccum lines, new EGR valve, new head gaskets, intake manifold gaskets and valve cover gaskets. I had a valve job done on the heads. I dont see any fuel leaks, or any other fluids for that matter. The car runs great, but bad milage. Now, I admit I have a horrible lead foot, but should that give me this bad of gas milage? New plugs, wires, cap and rotor also. No computer codes are present at all. Stupid question, but could it be a sensor maybe? Which one would be likely? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||pontiacs4me||03-29-2001||01:51 AM||||I drive my car like im in an Indy car race, and I get at least 19 mpg in the city.  Try a new O2 sensor, did you replace your fuel injectors when you did your rebuild?  My engine has 116K on it and with a new egr valve gasket, some plugs and wires and a o2 sensor it runs great.  Try that first.||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||03-29-2001||05:44 AM||||I agree, change the O2 sensor -- the car will run rich until you do -- I change mine once a year when I do a tune-up.......||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||03-29-2001||02:04 PM||||Also put in a new coolant temp sensor and MAT sensor.  The computer may think your car is not warming up, and giving you a really rich mixture.  My GT got about 10mpg right after I got it, after a good tune-up (plugs, wires, air filter, fuel filter, 02 sensor, coolant temp sensor, cap and rotor, ignition coil, and ignition pick-up) and a few cans of seafoam (don't know if they helped all that much) I'm getting 20mpg average on my 156,000 mile engine.  Even better if I do alot of highway driving.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||03-29-2001||04:16 PM||||DragonBrakes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?  Worth checking  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000005||Seanh||03-29-2001||06:29 PM||||I got a new O2 sensor today and will put it in tonight and see if it does any good. I have a new coolant temp sensor installed already and its still getting bad milage. But, I haven't replace the MAT sensor at all. That could be the problem. Thanks<P>Dragon brakes? The only working brake right now is the front driver side. Rears are just worn, and the passenger side is locked, but not putting drag on the wheel(I know, I checked it last night). At the time I'm not to sad about gas milage, Im am just so happy its nice outside so I can drive it. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark||03-29-2001||08:14 PM||||Do you have the stock (195 degree) thermostat installed?  The engine has to get up to normal operating temp else it runs rich.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||03-29-2001||11:55 PM||||Yes, I have the stock 195 thermostat in. The car used to run right at 195-220, but since I rebuilt the engine it seem to run cooler, about 165 usally, unless I race then it gets up to around 195-210. Probally since alot of the coolant passages were plugged up, and needed to be cleaned out. I justed replaced the O2 sensor, and drove it a little. Tried to get the MAT sensor, but my local Advance Auto parts clerk was a little stupid, and told me (I saw this listed on his screen) "Manifold Temperature Sensor" or somthing like that. He told me this is the coolant temp sensor, but I told him I had bought one of those also, and it was listed "Coolant Temp sensor". Geez these guys are stupid. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||03-30-2001||04:47 AM||||Before you go any further, make sure you do a complete tune up. That means replacing the pick-up coil and module in the distributer. I always use AC/Delco parts for those ignition parts. If THAT doesn't do anything for your mileage then go on to the sensors. But you should immediately notice that your Fiero will start on the first try after rebuilding your distributor. <P>Oh and the ignition coil if it hasn't already been replaced!<P>Let us know what worked!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-30-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||DRH||03-30-2001||09:53 AM||||Get a new thermostat. With a good thermostat the engine shouldn't run cool after the first few minutes of warm up. They're only a few bucks and real easy to replace. If it's running cool enough it could cause your mileage problem. The ECM will adjust to a rich mixture.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||03-30-2001||04:21 PM||||Well, I threw in a new O2 sensor and it seems to still get about the same milage. I have done a tune up, mostly. New cap, rotor, plugs, but didnt replace the wires, module, or pickup coil. I put in a new distributor about a year ago, so Im not sure if it needs all that, but it could't hurt. I havent replaced the coil either. What type of coil and module would you guys reccomend? Stock/AC, Accel, what? Thanks<P>I am going to get a thermostate tonight and put it in. I notice the car takes about 10-12 minutes at idle speed to get to normal operating temp, and once its up there is swings around from 160-235 degrees. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||03-30-2001||10:42 PM||||I got and installed a new thermostate today, and the car seems to run cooler now!<P>First off, logic would tell me that the largest mark, between the 100` and 220` mark, would be 160` right? The car seems to run just barly above 160` if thats what that mark is. How many degrees does each mark equal? How long after start-up should the engine be at normal operating temperature?<P>BTW, both new and old thermostats are 195 deg. stats.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||03-31-2001||01:15 PM||||Check over the ACT/MAT sensor in the air cleaner and it's wires. That could hurt milage if it's not passing a good reading to the ECM. The V6 isn't as picky about it as the 87+ 4 cyls but it's definatly something to check. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||03-31-2001||11:14 PM||||I don't know why you are getting such lousy gas milage unless your shift points are at 5500 rpm.  I just got back from the Daytona trip 2973 miles getting 31.6 mpg.  All highway at 65-75 mph.  I had a new distributor put in last year, a tune up was last done in 1996 30000 miles ago. New EGR valve in 98, egr solinoid prior to the Daytona trip (code 32).  I still have the original coil. The thottle body and intake were decarbonized last year, but I was still getting 30+ mpg prior to that.  Since I bought the car new, it has always gotten in the low 30 mpg over the road.<P>Although the check engine light popped on when I started out for Daytona, it never came back in the last week and a half, and 2500 miles.  Maybe the solinoid was the answer.||||reg||4
Z||000014||Will||03-31-2001||11:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR>Also put in a new coolant temp sensor and MAT sensor.  The computer may think your car is not warming up, and giving you a really rich mixture.  My GT got about 10mpg right after I got it, after a good tune-up (plugs, wires, air filter, fuel filter, 02 sensor, coolant temp sensor, cap and rotor, ignition coil, and ignition pick-up) and a few cans of seafoam (don't know if they helped all that much) I'm getting 20mpg average on my 156,000 mile engine.  Even better if I do alot of highway driving.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Did you replace all that stuff at once? If so, how can you possibly know what fixed the problem?<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I agree, change the O2 sensor -- the car will run rich until you do -- I change mine once a year when I do a tune-up.......<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's not necessary. Before my 14 yr old oxygen sensor started acting up recently, I had seen as high as 31 mpg hwy with my V6.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||04-01-2001||12:11 AM||||My baby is an Automatic, no 5500rmp shift points for me.<P>I am currently replacing the module, pick-up, and ignition coil. Well see what this does for me. I'll keep this updated next time I fill up and see what kinda milage I get.||||reg||1
Z||000016||88formula||04-01-2001||01:30 AM||||Just guessing and replacing parts is not going to solve your problem unless you get lucky. It's just going to drain your wallet. Some of the most common and simple problems with these cars that cause poor gas mileage are exhaust leaks that are ahead of the O2 sensor and vacuum leaks. Get a manual the start doing some diagnostic work before you run out of money.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||04-01-2001||01:38 AM||||Well, I only replaced the coil, pickup, module, and O2 sensor so far. I'll tell ya, that coil was the best thing I ever bought. That really improved engine performance. My old one must have been shot. I even had the set the idle down when I put the new coil on. I might still need to tighen up my manifold to y-pipe bolts, but there pretty snug already. I have welded (and tested for leaks) manifolds, and new exhaust gaskets.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Greg Piet||04-01-2001||05:32 AM||||camshaft?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Seanh||04-01-2001||09:19 AM||||Oh Please don't Say That!||||reg||1
