Z||000021||WKDFIRO||04-02-2001||02:05 AM||||Egads! <P>Call me madder than a British Cow if Im just relaying what has worked in my Fiero and what I have seen working at a shop called Fiero Motorsports!'' Unfortunately, the good Fiero manuals are starting to get hard to find!<P>I disagree with some of the suggestions that are made on this Forum but I wouldn't be foolish enough to disregard them outright!<BR>I was under the impression that if Sean asked us what we thought that he  would also be bright enough to also know that its free advice and sometimes worth every nickel!<P> ||||reg||10
Z||000022||Greg Piet||04-02-2001||04:54 AM||||Arent things like exhaust leaks, and vaccum leaks pretty OBVIOUS!?!?<P>I am figuring before someone goes out and buys ridiculously priced sensors, gets thier heads re-done, etc...  they would fix such things as an exhaust leak.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Seanh||04-02-2001||08:17 AM||||Well, just to let you guys know, since Iv replaced the coil, module, and pickup, had a 3/4 tank when I did this, I averaged 15.8 mpg on this tank. Thats definatly an improvment. But the damn thermostat is still running it pretty cool, actually too cool consider its a 195 stat and opens around 160. I'm taking it back today and getting a new one.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Old Lar||04-02-2001||01:36 PM||||My car was running "cool" 170 degrees, I changed to a new 195 thermostat, but it still runs the same.  When I get stuck in traffic, the temp climbs up, but once rolling the car cools off.  It still runs great with good milage.  Mine started running cool when I replaced the cat and put on a Borla exhaust.  I just chalked the temp change to the exhaust change, cause I don't know what else was done to reflect a reduced running temp.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||3
